Lazy loading of weights in the Vector-oriented Latent Variable Model (vLLM) is a technique that delays the loading of weights during the training process until they are actually needed. This approach reduces the memory requirements of the model and speeds up the initial training phase.

In vLLM, the weights are organized into layers, and each layer has its own set of weights. The weights in later layers are more complex and rely on the weights in earlier layers. However, during the early stages of training, the model can learn effectively without these more complex weights, as they represent higher-level abstractions. Therefore, the method of lazy loading allows the model to focus on the simpler weights first, reducing the computational cost and improving the training efficiency.

When a weight is needed during the forward pass or backpropagation, it is loaded from memory, and the corresponding computation is performed. This reduces the storage requirements significantly but still allows the model to learn the complex relationships between the weights and achieve reasonable performance. Thus, lazy loading of weights in vLLM is an effective memory management strategy that aids in scaling deep learning models to larger datasets and more layers.
