 Residual connections refer to a technique used in some deep learning models, including the transformer architecture, to mitigate the problem of vanishing gradients. In a transformer model, the residual connections allow the training process to flow more efficiently by adding the input to the output of each layer, effectively skipping some layers. This way, the key, query, and value matrices are updated while still preserving the raw input and helping the model learn more effectively and converge faster. This technique is crucial in deep, complex models like transformers to ensure the error signal propagates effectively throughout the network and avoids the vanishing gradient problem.
