FP32 is natively supported by most popular frameworks and hardware architectures, making it the default choice for model training and inferenceTasks.

### Float16 (16-bit floating-point)

Microsoft developed the Float16 (half-precision floating-point) format to cater to applications that can tolerate lower precision in exchange for lower compute, storage, and power requirements. Float16 uses 5 bits for the exponent, 10 bits for the mantissa, and 1 bit for the sign. Its value range is from 5e-4 to 6e+4 with a relative error of 9E–4.

Support for Float16 has been widespread across machine learning frameworks (e.g., Tensorflow, PyTorch) and hardware accelerators (e.g., GPUs, TPUs). Using Float16 can typically lead to model size reductions of 1.5x and faster inference speeds by utilizing hardware optimized for half-precision computations.

