`vLLM` ordinarily streams its `POST` responses for the `auth`, `chat`, and `stream` APIs. This allows `vLLM` to provide a high-performance, low memory-consumption service for these actions. It does, however, complicate things for Go programmers who are not familiar with working with streaming responses.

The following Go code demonstrates how to parse a `POST` response as a streaming response with the `io/ioutil` Go package:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"io"
	"net/http"
)

const url = "https://api.ourai.ai/vllm/api/auth"

func main() {
	resp, err := http.Post(url, "application/x-www-form-urlencoded", nil)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println("GET:", err)
		return
	}

	defer resp.Body.Close()

	bytes, err := io.ReadAll(resp.Body)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println("Read:", err)
		return
	}

	fmt.Println("RESPONSE:", string(bytes))
}
```

Additionally, you can process each chunk of data as it arrives if you need to work with the response more dynamically:

```go
func main() {
	resp, err := http.Post(url, "application/x-www-form-urlencoded", nil)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println("GET:", err)
		return
	}

