🧠 LLM Response:


There is no direct way to send multiple prompts in a single request to the API. However, you can use separation characters (currently only TAB) to create a multi-prompt response.

Here's an example of how you can use it:

Assuming you have three text inputs, prompt1, prompt2, and prompt3, you can write your function like this:

```javascript
async function multiPrompt(model, prompt1, prompt2, prompt3) {
    const input = prompt1 + '\t' + prompt2 + '\t' + prompt3;
    const response = await OpenAI.Completion.create({
        model: model,
        prompt: input,
        temperature: 0.9,
        max_tokens: 1024,
        top_p: 1,
        frequency_penalty: 0.3,
        presence_penalty: 0.3,
    });

    // Split the response using TAB character (tabs come before newlines)
    const responses = response.choices[0].text.split('\n\t');

    // Extract the relevant responses. In this example, we assume the responses
    // should be the first lines of each answer. But if multiple answers are
    // possible, you may need to handle this a bit more carefully.
    const results = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < responses.length; i += 2) {
        results.push(responses[i]);
    }

    return results;
}
```

Now you can call `multiPrompt` with your prompts:

```javascript
const result = await multiPrompt('text-davinci-003', "Who is the current president of France?", "How many continents are there in the world?", "What's the meaning of life?");
console.log(result); // Logs: [ "Emmanuel Macron", "There are 7 continents" ]
```

Please note that this method may not always guarantee getting multiple answers in the same order as the prompts due to the stochastic nature of the model. For more deterministic results, you can structure the prompt explicitly.

In this example, we're only returning the first line of the response since the prompts aren't flexible to handle multi-paragraph answers. If you need to handle more complex responses, make sure to adapt the function to handle them accordingly.
